raw:
Comparing the two pages, the only difference is the order of the three h3 items under "Why people switch to Northbrew". The content is identical, so the reordering doesn't matter.

I also checked for contradictions in both pages. Each one says $24 per bottle, 16 drinks and 15% off, which gives $20.40. Shipping is free over $50 and otherwise $5.95. The mixing ratio is one part concentrate to two parts water, so 32 oz of concentrate makes about 96 oz of drink, or 16 drinks of 6 oz. That is consistent.

{"better": "same", "problem": "none", "evidence": "The pages differ only in the order of the three benefit blocks, and the content is otherwise identical."}

Comparing the pages, the only difference is the order of the three benefit blocks under "Why people switch to Northbrew". Page A lists "Less than a cafe", "Keeps for weeks", then "Smooth, never bitter". Page B lists them in the reverse order. The wording is identical, so this does not affect sign-ups, sales or trust.

Both pages are internally consistent. The 16 drinks at 2 parts water to 1 part concentrate math is roughly fine (32 oz bottle, 2 oz per drink would be 16 drinks; 1 part to 2 parts water gives 6 oz drinks, so 2 oz concentrate each). The subscription price of $20.40 is 15% off $24, which is correct. Neither page has a problem the other lacks.

{"better": "same", "problem": "none", "evidence": "The pages are identical except for the order of the three benefit blocks, which does not matter."}

The only difference is that Page A has a signup dialog (a newsletter offer for a free guide) that appears when the page opens. In the text it comes last, but it is marked as a dialog, so it is probably an overlay. It has a close button (×) and a "No thanks" link, so it can be dismissed. Even so, a modal that covers the main content on load is a common conversion problem. Page B has the same content without the dialog.

Whether this counts as blocking depends on the dialog actually appearing on load. It is labeled "Signup offer" and listed as a dialog, so I'll treat it as a blocking popup. Page B is better.

{"better": "B", "problem": "blocking_popup", "evidence": "Page A includes a 'Signup offer' dialog for a free guide that overlays the page, while Page B has no popup."}
